WEBVTT

00:00:01.500 --> 00:00:05.670
The ocean covers most of our planet, yet we know very little about it.
海洋覆盖了我们这个星球的大部分面积，然而我们对它知之甚少。

00:00:06.470 --> 00:00:09.290
Our ocean is as mysterious as it is beautiful.
我们的海洋有多美丽，就有多神秘。

00:00:10.090 --> 00:00:14.440
In fact, we have better maps of Mars than we do of the ocean floor.
事实上，我们的火星地图比海底地图还要好。

00:00:15.240 --> 00:00:15.840
Why?
为什么呢？

00:00:16.640 --> 00:00:19.190
Exploring the ocean is a really hard job.
探索海洋是一项非常艰难的工作。

00:00:19.990 --> 00:00:24.240
There’s a lot of ocean—about 1.4 billion trillion litres.
海洋的水量非常大——大约有14万亿亿升。

00:00:25.040 --> 00:00:27.720
That’s 1,400,000,000,000,000,000,000!
也就是1,400,000,000,000,000,000,000！

00:00:28.520 --> 00:00:30.850
But many scientists are taking on this challenge.
但许多科学家正在迎接这一挑战。

00:00:31.650 --> 00:00:38.000
One of their most important goals is to learn about coral reefs, which are home to lots of sea plants and animals.
他们最重要的目标之一是了解珊瑚礁，珊瑚礁是许多海洋动植物的家园。

00:00:38.800 --> 00:00:41.820
Do you know that coral is an animal, not a plant?
你知道珊瑚是动物，而不是植物吗？

00:00:42.620 --> 00:00:47.340
Tiny animals called polyps build the hard part of the coral as a home to live in.
一种叫作珊瑚虫的微小动物建造出珊瑚坚硬的部分，当作自己的家，住在里面。

00:00:48.140 --> 00:00:50.220
They are called “architects” in the ocean.
它们被称为海洋中的“建筑师”。

00:00:51.020 --> 00:00:54.210
However, many coral reefs around the world are in danger.
然而，世界各地有许多珊瑚礁正处于危险之中。

00:00:55.010 --> 00:01:00.260
That’s where a clever method called “coral gardening” comes in—giving reefs a second chance.
这时，一种叫作“珊瑚园艺”的巧妙方法就派上用场了——它给珊瑚礁第二次机会。

00:01:01.060 --> 00:01:02.880
Here’s how it works in four steps:
下面就是它的运作方式，共分四步：

00:01:03.680 --> 00:01:07.350
First, scientists collect small pieces of coral from coral reefs.
首先，科学家从珊瑚礁上采集小块珊瑚。

00:01:08.150 --> 00:01:12.330
Then they break them into even smaller ones—tiny pieces grow faster.
然后，他们把这些小块分成更小的碎块——细小的碎块长得更快。

00:01:13.130 --> 00:01:20.530
After that, they place the pieces in lab-controlled or underwater nurseries, where tiny pieces are taken good care of.
之后，他们把这些碎块放进由实验室控制的苗圃或水下苗圃，细小的碎块在那里会得到精心照料。

00:01:21.330 --> 00:01:27.390
Finally, once strong enough, they are planted onto rock or man-made reefs so that they can grow on their own.
最后，碎块一旦长得足够健壮，就会被种到岩石或人造礁上，这样它们就能自己生长了。

00:01:28.190 --> 00:01:30.620
A typical example is the L-shaped Reef.
一个典型的例子是L形礁。

00:01:31.420 --> 00:01:36.110
Since 1982, scientists have placed old subway cars into the sea.
自1982年以来，科学家们已经陆续把旧地铁车厢放进了海里。

00:01:36.910 --> 00:01:40.020
These strange “homes” have created a huge man-made reef.
这些奇特的“家”已经形成了一片巨大的人造礁。

00:01:40.820 --> 00:01:42.680
Now, polyps and fish are moving in!
现在，珊瑚虫和鱼儿正在搬进来！

00:01:43.480 --> 00:01:50.560
To know more about the ocean, a volunteer, Paul Green, started the Big Bag Project in 2025.
为了更多地了解海洋，志愿者保罗·格林于2025年发起了“大袋子项目”。

00:01:51.360 --> 00:01:58.860
It calls on beach visitors to help collect information on plastics and water temperature through a bag which is connected with an app.
该项目号召海滩游客通过一个与应用程序相连的袋子，帮忙收集有关塑料和水温的信息。

00:01:59.660 --> 00:02:05.200
Next time you go to the beach, take a bag, pick up some rubbish, and have a closer connection with the ocean.
下次你去海滩时，带上一个袋子，捡些垃圾，和海洋建立更紧密的联系吧。
